1
00:00:05,124 --> 00:00:06,707
Hi everyone, I'm Joy.

2
00:00:06,707 --> 00:00:08,138
Today, I'd like to show you

3
00:00:08,138 --> 00:00:11,101
How to make your app understandable to Apple Intelligence.

4
00:00:11,101 --> 00:00:12,723
First, let me briefly introduce myself.

5
00:00:12,723 --> 00:00:14,345
I currently work at eCloudMobile Crop.

6
00:00:14,345 --> 00:00:18,366
I've been part of the iPlayground staff for the past two years.

7
00:00:18,366 --> 00:00:20,230
And in my spare time, I'm also a YouTuber.

8
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:24,063
Now, let's get into today's topic.

9
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:28,451
Let's start by looking back at how users used to interact with Siri.

10
00:00:28,451 --> 00:00:31,841
When you asked Siri a question,

11
00:00:31,841 --> 00:00:34,430
It often couldn't really understand what you mean.

12
00:00:34,430 --> 00:00:37,990
And its answer was often different from what you expected.

13
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:43,642
Many times, it would simply send what you said to the web and search for it.

14
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:47,432
I'm sure many of you have already seen

15
00:00:47,432 --> 00:00:50,501
What Siri AI can do at WWDC26.

16
00:00:50,501 --> 00:00:52,536
It can now understand your app.

17
00:00:52,536 --> 00:00:57,560
In everyday use, we usually don't give Siri extremely precise instructions like,

18
00:00:57,560 --> 00:01:01,869
“Please add the iPlayground event to my calendar.”

19
00:01:01,869 --> 00:01:03,043
More often,

20
00:01:03,043 --> 00:01:04,723
We would simply say to Siri,

21
00:01:04,723 --> 00:01:07,355
“Please add this event to my calendar.”

22
00:01:07,355 --> 00:01:08,901
And here's the question:

23
00:01:08,901 --> 00:01:11,381
How does it know what “this” refers to?

24
00:01:11,381 --> 00:01:14,526
Siri needs to truly understand what the user wants.

25
00:01:14,526 --> 00:01:18,496
And it also needs to understand what we're currently looking at.

26
00:01:18,754 --> 00:01:22,070
The thing is, Siri doesn't actually understand your UI.

27
00:01:22,385 --> 00:01:24,861
What it understands is your data.

28
00:01:25,190 --> 00:01:28,574
So we need to make our app understandable to Siri.

29
00:01:29,072 --> 00:01:30,544
We need to

30
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:34,669
Expose the underlying data in our app in a way Siri can understand.

31
00:01:34,669 --> 00:01:36,029
Otherwise, from Siri's perspective,

32
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:38,139
Your app is basically a black box.

33
00:01:38,139 --> 00:01:41,597
It can see the screen, but it doesn't know what the content actually represents.

34
00:01:41,597 --> 00:01:44,870
And it doesn't know what actions it can perform.

35
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:47,395
So we need to take the data inside our app

36
00:01:47,395 --> 00:01:51,094
And structure it in a way the system can understand.

37
00:01:52,021 --> 00:01:54,812
This brings us to App Entity.

38
00:01:54,812 --> 00:01:58,149
App Entity is a way to represent structured data

39
00:01:58,149 --> 00:02:00,295
In a form that Siri can understand.

40
00:02:00,295 --> 00:02:02,976
You can think of it as a type.

41
00:02:02,976 --> 00:02:07,310
If you have a calendar, you might have an Event Entity.

42
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:09,518
If you have Mail, you might have a Mail Entity.

43
00:02:09,518 --> 00:02:12,113
If you have an album, you might have a Photo Entity.

44
00:02:12,113 --> 00:02:13,849
Of course, each type has instances:

45
00:02:13,849 --> 00:02:17,716
Event 1, Event 2, Mail 1, Mail 2, and so on.

46
00:02:17,716 --> 00:02:20,806
So you need to model your data as App Entities.

47
00:02:20,806 --> 00:02:23,600
But simply turning your data into App Entities isn't enough.

48
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:28,916
You also need to define them more clearly,

49
00:02:28,916 --> 00:02:30,373
So the system knows exactly what they mean.

50
00:02:30,987 --> 00:02:33,331
I define an Event Entity

51
00:02:33,331 --> 00:02:35,779
With fields like title, date, and content.

52
00:02:35,779 --> 00:02:38,328
When we see these fields,

53
00:02:38,328 --> 00:02:40,590
We immediately think, “Okay, this represents an event.”

54
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:42,969
But Siri doesn't automatically understand that.

55
00:02:42,969 --> 00:02:46,287
It only sees a String and doesn't know what that String represents.

56
00:02:46,287 --> 00:02:49,590
It doesn't know whether your date is a creation date or a due date.

57
00:02:50,169 --> 00:02:53,495
That's where App Schemas come in.

58
00:02:53,495 --> 00:02:56,202
App Schemas describe the semantics of your entities.

59
00:02:56,202 --> 00:02:58,491
They tell Siri, “This is an event.”

60
00:02:58,491 --> 00:03:00,286
“This field represents the title.”

61
00:03:00,286 --> 00:03:03,380
“And this field represents the due date.”

62
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:06,160
That allows Siri to understand your data

63
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,385
And reason about how to use it later.

64
00:03:10,718 --> 00:03:14,733
Apple has already defined

65
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:16,899
A whole set of semantic models through App Schemas.

66
00:03:16,899 --> 00:03:20,952
Today, I'll use Reminders as my example.

67
00:03:20,952 --> 00:03:23,236
The Reminders structure is relatively straightforward,

68
00:03:23,236 --> 00:03:25,892
So it's easier to understand.

69
00:03:25,892 --> 00:03:31,034
You can find the other schemas in Apple's official documentation.

70
00:03:31,590 --> 00:03:34,840
In the Reminders App Schema domain,

71
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:36,935
Apple has already predefined many

72
00:03:36,935 --> 00:03:39,866
Actions and types.

73
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:41,961
In practice,

74
00:03:41,961 --> 00:03:44,233
You use them through a hierarchical structure.

75
00:03:44,233 --> 00:03:46,762
First, you have the App Schema domain,

76
00:03:46,762 --> 00:03:49,711
Followed by the schema type.

77
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,354
So when you define

78
00:03:53,715 --> 00:03:58,313
Your ReminderEntity using the reminder type,

79
00:03:58,313 --> 00:04:01,240
There are a number of required properties

80
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:04,023
That Apple has already defined and that you need to implement.

81
00:04:04,023 --> 00:04:05,983
Otherwise, you'll get an error.

82
00:04:05,983 --> 00:04:08,543
You need to apply the schema

83
00:04:08,543 --> 00:04:11,584
And implement all of its required properties.

84
00:04:11,888 --> 00:04:13,964
One particularly important property is

85
00:04:13,964 --> 00:04:14,899
The ID.

86
00:04:14,899 --> 00:04:18,277
The ID is used to uniquely identify an entity.

87
00:04:18,710 --> 00:04:21,566
There's another very important part:

88
00:04:21,566 --> 00:04:22,371
The query.

89
00:04:22,371 --> 00:04:26,895
You can think of a query like the query you use to fetch data from a database.

90
00:04:26,895 --> 00:04:28,112
What does that mean?

91
00:04:28,112 --> 00:04:30,272
Siri may need to come back into our app

92
00:04:30,272 --> 00:04:32,449
And retrieve a specific entity.

93
00:04:32,449 --> 00:04:34,718
To do that, it needs a query.

94
00:04:34,718 --> 00:04:36,297
There are usually two ways to query.

95
00:04:36,297 --> 00:04:37,876
One is by ID,

96
00:04:37,876 --> 00:04:40,128
And the other is by a String.

97
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:42,976
So the overall flow looks like this.

98
00:04:42,976 --> 00:04:45,588
Siri first performs resolution.

99
00:04:45,588 --> 00:04:48,200
It tries to understand what the user is asking for.

100
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:50,783
Then, through App Intents,

101
00:04:50,783 --> 00:04:53,257
It reaches into your app and retrieves the entity.

102
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:58,195
Earlier, we said that schemas define the meaning of your data.

103
00:04:58,195 --> 00:05:03,201
App Intents describe the capabilities your app can provide

104
00:05:03,201 --> 00:05:05,817
And the actions it can perform.

105
00:05:06,602 --> 00:05:10,752
If we create a regular custom App Intent,

106
00:05:10,752 --> 00:05:13,072
For example,  FeedMyDogIntent,

107
00:05:13,072 --> 00:05:16,647
Siri doesn't inherently know what “feed” means here, or what “dog” refers to.

108
00:05:16,647 --> 00:05:18,390
So it doesn't know how or when to use that intent.

109
00:05:18,390 --> 00:05:19,847
In that case,

110
00:05:21,268 --> 00:05:25,282
With a regular custom definition,

111
00:05:25,282 --> 00:05:28,849
You can expose it through Shortcuts or Spotlight for users to invoke,

112
00:05:28,849 --> 00:05:31,450
But Siri itself doesn't understand its semantics.

113
00:05:32,353 --> 00:05:33,782
(Exhales)

114
00:05:33,782 --> 00:05:37,059
App Entity itself isn't a new technology.

115
00:05:37,059 --> 00:05:40,632
It's already widely used across the system.

116
00:05:40,632 --> 00:05:44,654
At WWDC26, the newest part isn't App Entity.

117
00:05:44,654 --> 00:05:46,919
The new concept we just discussed is App Schemas.

118
00:05:46,919 --> 00:05:49,862
With App Schemas,

119
00:05:49,862 --> 00:05:54,979
An intent can be understood in terms of when and how it should be used.

120
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:58,110
Apple provides these predefined App Schemas,

121
00:05:58,110 --> 00:06:01,285
And we need to follow their existing specifications.

122
00:06:01,824 --> 00:06:04,114
So today, I'll define an App Intent.

123
00:06:04,114 --> 00:06:07,835
I want to define a CreateIntent.

124
00:06:07,835 --> 00:06:12,392
I can define its schema type as createReminder.

125
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:16,552
The most important part of an App Intent is perform.

126
00:06:16,552 --> 00:06:18,627
When a user makes a request,

127
00:06:18,627 --> 00:06:21,228
For example, “Create a reminder for me,”

128
00:06:21,228 --> 00:06:23,218
The system executes perform.

129
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:27,129
Now let me introduce this using my sample app.

130
00:06:27,129 --> 00:06:29,978
I'll probably release this app in the future.

131
00:06:29,978 --> 00:06:33,748
It's a reminders app, and this is its home page.

132
00:06:33,748 --> 00:06:37,457
In the middle, there are four categories:

133
00:06:37,457 --> 00:06:40,394
Work, Personal, Social, and Study.

134
00:06:40,394 --> 00:06:46,187
Each category contains its corresponding to-do items.

135
00:06:46,187 --> 00:06:49,283
And of course, you can open each item to view its details.

136
00:06:49,283 --> 00:06:51,658
That's the current structure of the app.

137
00:06:52,161 --> 00:06:55,544
After implementing CreateIntent,

138
00:06:55,544 --> 00:06:58,251
We can interact with Siri using natural language.

139
00:06:58,251 --> 00:06:59,620
For example, I can say to Siri,

140
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:04,462
“Create a to-do for preparing my slides, under the Personal category.”

141
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:08,368
Siri understands the meaning of my request

142
00:07:08,368 --> 00:07:12,723
And invokes CreateIntent to create the item for me.

143
00:07:12,990 --> 00:07:14,348
And this action

144
00:07:14,348 --> 00:07:18,080
Whether your app is in the foreground, in the background, or has been terminated.

145
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:20,562
Siri can still complete the action.

146
00:07:20,951 --> 00:07:26,202
Earlier, I didn't specify a due date to Siri.

147
00:07:26,202 --> 00:07:28,806
So I need to update the item.

148
00:07:28,806 --> 00:07:30,541
For that, I created an UpdateIntent.

149
00:07:30,541 --> 00:07:33,972
UpdateIntent works similarly to the CreateIntent we just saw.

150
00:07:33,972 --> 00:07:35,855
So I ask Siri to update it,

151
00:07:36,125 --> 00:07:41,592
And it successfully changes the due date to tomorrow.

152
00:07:41,592 --> 00:07:44,982
In my testing, the data is indeed updated correctly.

153
00:07:44,982 --> 00:07:47,141
But Siri currently responds with,

154
00:07:47,141 --> 00:07:50,217
“Something went wrong. Please try again.”

155
00:07:50,217 --> 00:07:54,435
The data update succeeds, but Siri still reports an error.

156
00:07:54,435 --> 00:07:57,769
So at this stage, the overall Siri workflow does work,

157
00:07:57,769 --> 00:08:02,483
But the experience is still not stable enough.

158
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:06,669
Next is one of the most important features introduced at WWDC26:

159
00:08:06,669 --> 00:08:08,950
On-screen Awareness.

160
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:10,699
This is Siri's ability to understand what's on your screen.

161
00:08:10,699 --> 00:08:14,962
In the past, Siri had very limited understanding of on-screen content.

162
00:08:14,962 --> 00:08:18,044
It mainly relied on OCR to interpret what was displayed.

163
00:08:18,044 --> 00:08:23,406
Apple's own apps could provide additional information to the system.

164
00:08:23,690 --> 00:08:26,437
With On-screen Awareness,

165
00:08:26,437 --> 00:08:29,670
Siri AI not only knows which entities are on the screen,

166
00:08:29,670 --> 00:08:32,609
It can even understand where those entities appear.

167
00:08:32,609 --> 00:08:35,005
So beyond simply recognizing them,

168
00:08:35,005 --> 00:08:38,571
It can use context to answer your questions.

169
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:40,751
Let's use my app as an example.

170
00:08:40,751 --> 00:08:45,173
With OCR alone, Siri can read the text on the screen.

171
00:08:45,173 --> 00:08:46,515
I have several tags here:

172
00:08:46,515 --> 00:08:50,161
Personal, High, and Active.

173
00:08:50,161 --> 00:08:53,774
But with OCR alone, Siri has no idea what those labels actually mean.

174
00:08:54,030 --> 00:08:58,911
So I need to use the App Entity definitions I created earlier to provide that context to the system.

175
00:08:58,911 --> 00:08:59,976
How do we do that?

176
00:08:59,976 --> 00:09:03,056
There are three approaches, depending on the complexity of your screen.

177
00:09:03,927 --> 00:09:08,904
The first is NSUserActivity.

178
00:09:08,904 --> 00:09:12,109
This works well when the screen is relatively simple, such as a detail page,

179
00:09:12,109 --> 00:09:14,630
Where you may only have one entity on the screen.

180
00:09:14,630 --> 00:09:17,150
In that case, you can use NSUserActivity.

181
00:09:17,150 --> 00:09:20,139
For example, I have a to-do item called “Walk the Shiba Inu on a typhoon day.”

182
00:09:20,139 --> 00:09:23,360
Although on a typhoon day, the Shiba Inu might be the one walking me!

183
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:25,384
The implementation is very simple.

184
00:09:25,384 --> 00:09:29,631
You create an NSUserActivity,

185
00:09:29,631 --> 00:09:34,950
And associate your entity and its ID with that NSUserActivity.

186
00:09:34,950 --> 00:09:36,386
And that's it.

187
00:09:37,139 --> 00:09:39,754
Once that's done, you can see that

188
00:09:39,754 --> 00:09:43,820
I ask Siri, “What is this?”

189
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:46,601
Siri is no longer just reading the text visible on the screen.

190
00:09:46,601 --> 00:09:50,140
It knows the title is “Walk the Shiba Inu on a typhoon day,”

191
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:52,348
The category is Personal,

192
00:09:52,348 --> 00:09:54,049
And the priority is High.

193
00:09:54,049 --> 00:09:57,501
It can understand all of this information from the entity.

194
00:09:59,396 --> 00:10:03,706
The second approach is for screens with many views and entities.

195
00:10:03,706 --> 00:10:06,762
You can use View Annotation.

196
00:10:06,762 --> 00:10:09,761
It's very simple: take the corresponding view

197
00:10:09,761 --> 00:10:13,586
And associate it with the corresponding entity and ID.

198
00:10:14,270 --> 00:10:16,481
But when you're working with a list,

199
00:10:16,481 --> 00:10:18,140
You might have a thousand views.

200
00:10:18,140 --> 00:10:20,980
You obviously can't annotate every single view.

201
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:25,117
That could introduce additional performance overhead.

202
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:28,856
And when a cell is reused,

203
00:10:28,856 --> 00:10:31,937
Its annotation may disappear with it.

204
00:10:31,937 --> 00:10:36,509
Apple provides Collection Annotation

205
00:10:36,509 --> 00:10:39,526
Specifically to handle large amounts of dynamic data.

206
00:10:39,526 --> 00:10:43,710
The idea is that you describe the entire list to Siri at once.

207
00:10:43,710 --> 00:10:50,495
You're essentially saying, “Here's my data. Ask me for the item you need when you need it.”

208
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:54,006
The implementation is straightforward.

209
00:10:54,006 --> 00:10:58,590
It's similar to how we normally implement a data source.

210
00:10:58,590 --> 00:11:03,570
Based on the indexPath, you simply return the corresponding entity and ID.

211
00:11:04,850 --> 00:11:07,464
So once my list supports this,

212
00:11:07,464 --> 00:11:10,726
I can say, “Update the second item on the screen.”

213
00:11:10,726 --> 00:11:12,990
And as you can see,

214
00:11:13,247 --> 00:11:14,845
The update succeeds.

215
00:11:14,845 --> 00:11:16,538
But the response still reports an error,

216
00:11:16,538 --> 00:11:19,349
Because the current experience is still somewhat unstable.

217
00:11:20,290 --> 00:11:22,803
Another important capability is Transferable.

218
00:11:22,803 --> 00:11:26,510
It allows you to pass your data to another app.

219
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:30,403
I have a to-do item called “Pick up package,”

220
00:11:30,403 --> 00:11:32,637
And I want to send it to my family.

221
00:11:32,637 --> 00:11:35,298
I simply conform the entity to the Transferable protocol.

222
00:11:35,298 --> 00:11:39,822
Then, in transferRepresentation,

223
00:11:40,105 --> 00:11:42,231
I expose the information I want to transfer.

224
00:11:42,231 --> 00:11:43,123
It's very simple.

225
00:11:43,123 --> 00:11:45,245
And now that data can be passed to another app.

226
00:11:45,245 --> 00:11:48,069
In this example, I'm sending it to Messages.

227
00:11:48,854 --> 00:11:50,913
Okay...

228
00:11:50,913 --> 00:11:52,938
What I really want to talk about is

229
00:11:52,938 --> 00:11:56,350
The past, present, and future of Siri.

230
00:11:56,350 --> 00:12:00,468
In the past, Siri treated your app as a destination.

231
00:12:00,468 --> 00:12:04,154
Your app was the endpoint where the task stopped.

232
00:12:04,550 --> 00:12:08,287
With today's Siri, you can think of it more like n8n.

233
00:12:08,287 --> 00:12:10,986
It acts as a workflow.

234
00:12:10,986 --> 00:12:14,097
Siri can retrieve data from one step

235
00:12:14,097 --> 00:12:18,468
And pass data from your app into the next app.

236
00:12:19,387 --> 00:12:21,027
If we zoom out and look at the bigger picture,

237
00:12:21,027 --> 00:12:24,530
We already know AI can help us do a huge range of things.

238
00:12:24,530 --> 00:12:28,290
Frontend, backend, client development—we can increasingly build all of these ourselves.

239
00:12:28,290 --> 00:12:32,050
AI can help us complete an entire development pipeline.

240
00:12:32,050 --> 00:12:36,440
If we apply the same idea to apps,

241
00:12:36,793 --> 00:12:38,349
This is the future I imagine.

242
00:12:38,349 --> 00:12:41,198
In the future, I don't think we'll only be building a single app.

243
00:12:41,198 --> 00:12:44,077
We may be building an entire app ecosystem.

244
00:12:44,077 --> 00:12:47,027
Each of these nodes could come from your own products.

245
00:12:47,027 --> 00:12:51,398
They can share consistent data and deliver a consistent experience,

246
00:12:51,398 --> 00:12:54,090
While working together seamlessly.

247
00:12:54,090 --> 00:12:57,619
Users may no longer depend on just one of your products,

248
00:12:57,619 --> 00:13:00,009
But on your entire product ecosystem.

249
00:13:00,009 --> 00:13:04,707
Perhaps the biggest change Apple Intelligence brings

250
00:13:04,707 --> 00:13:06,164
Isn't simply about making a single app smarter.

251
00:13:06,164 --> 00:13:09,880
It's about who can provide a complete set of capabilities

252
00:13:09,880 --> 00:13:13,885
That AI can understand, compose, and coordinate most effectively.

253
00:13:14,272 --> 00:13:16,591
So I think Siri AI...

254
00:13:16,847 --> 00:13:18,216
Sorry, I misspoke.

